---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="guides/finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Quick Answer -->
  <h2 id="quick-answer">Quick Answer: Yes, With the Right Documentation</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Yes, home office furniture is tax deductible</strong>—if you're self-employed or have
    freelance income and use the furniture in a dedicated workspace for business.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can deduct the full cost immediately using Section 179, or depreciate it over several years.
    The key requirements:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You must be self-employed (employees generally can't deduct at federal level)</li>
    <li>The furniture must be used for business purposes</li>
    <li>You need proper documentation (receipts, photos)</li>
    <li>The home office must meet IRS requirements (regular and exclusive use)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Read on for the details on what qualifies, how to document it, and common mistakes to avoid.
  </p>

  <!-- What Furniture Qualifies -->
  <h2 id="what-qualifies">What Furniture Qualifies for Deduction</h2>

  <h3>Commonly Deductible Items</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Desks:</strong> <a href="/gear/best-standing-desks">Standing desks</a>, <a
        href="/gear/best-compact-desks-small-office">compact desks</a>, traditional desks
    </li>
    <li><strong>Chairs:</strong> <a href="/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs">Ergonomic chairs</a>, task chairs, kneeling
      chairs
    </li>
    <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Filing cabinets, bookcases, shelving</li>
    <li><strong>Lighting:</strong> Desk lamps, floor lamps (especially if task-specific)</li>
    <li><strong>Accessories:</strong> Monitor stands, keyboard trays, cable management</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What Doesn't Qualify</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Decorative items:</strong> Art, plants (unless integral to business)</li>
    <li><strong>Personal furniture:</strong> Items in shared spaces not exclusively for work</li>
    <li><strong>Kitchen items:</strong> Coffee maker, refrigerator (unless in dedicated office)</li>
    <li><strong>General home improvement:</strong> Painting, flooring (unless prorated for office)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Business Use Test</h3>
  <p>
    Ask yourself: "Would I have purchased this item if I didn't work from home?"
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>If yes → It's a personal expense, not deductible</li>
    <li>If no → It's likely a business expense</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A $1,500 ergonomic chair specifically for long work hours? Business expense. A comfortable
    couch where you sometimes answer emails? Personal expense.
  </p>

  <!-- Deduction Methods -->
  <h2 id="deduction-methods">Two Ways to Deduct: Immediate vs. Over Time</h2>

  <h3>Section 179: Immediate Deduction</h3>
  <p>
    Section 179 lets you deduct the <strong>full cost of business equipment</strong> in the year
    you buy it, rather than spreading it out over years.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>2024 limit:</strong> Up to $1,160,000 in total Section 179 deductions</li>
    <li><strong>Per item:</strong> No per-item limit for most furniture</li>
    <li><strong>When to use:</strong> When you want the full tax benefit now</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Example:</strong> You buy a $800 standing desk and $500 ergonomic chair in 2024.
    With Section 179, you deduct the full $1,300 on your 2024 taxes.
  </p>

  <h3>Depreciation: Spread Over Years</h3>
  <p>
    Instead of deducting everything at once, you can depreciate furniture over its
    "useful life" (typically 5-7 years for office furniture).
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Furniture depreciation period:</strong> 7 years (MACRS)</li>
    <li><strong>Annual deduction:</strong> Roughly 14% of cost per year</li>
    <li><strong>When to use:</strong> If you expect higher income in future years</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Example:</strong> A $700 desk depreciated over 7 years = ~$100 deduction per year.
  </p>

  <h3>Which Should You Choose?</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Most freelancers should use Section 179</strong> because:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Immediate tax savings are worth more than future savings (time value of money)</li>
    <li>Simpler record-keeping</li>
    <li>You likely need the deduction more now than later</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Choose depreciation if you have a low-income year and expect higher taxes later.
  </p>

  <!-- Depreciation Explained -->
  <h2 id="depreciation">How Depreciation Works</h2>

  <h3>MACRS Depreciation</h3>
  <p>
    The Modified Accelerated Cost Recovery System (MACRS) is the IRS method for depreciating
    business property. Office furniture is classified as 7-year property.
  </p>
  <p>
    With MACRS, you don't deduct an even amount each year. The percentages are:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Year 1: 14.29%</li>
    <li>Year 2: 24.49%</li>
    <li>Year 3: 17.49%</li>
    <li>Year 4: 12.49%</li>
    <li>Year 5: 8.93%</li>
    <li>Year 6: 8.92%</li>
    <li>Year 7: 8.93%</li>
    <li>Year 8: 4.46%</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Bonus Depreciation</h3>
  <p>
    In addition to MACRS, "bonus depreciation" allows deducting a large percentage upfront:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>2024:</strong> 60% bonus depreciation</li>
    <li><strong>2025:</strong> 40% bonus depreciation</li>
    <li><strong>2026:</strong> 20% bonus depreciation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This phases out by 2027. For most freelancers, Section 179 is simpler and achieves
    the same result (full immediate deduction).
  </p>

  <!-- Documentation Requirements -->
  <h2 id="documentation">Documentation Requirements</h2>

  <h3>What to Keep</h3>
  <p>
    For every furniture purchase you want to deduct:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Receipt or invoice:</strong> Shows item, date, amount paid</li>
    <li><strong>Proof of payment:</strong> Credit card statement, bank record</li>
    <li><strong>Photos:</strong> Picture of item in your home office</li>
    <li><strong>Business purpose:</strong> Brief note on why you needed it</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Home Office Documentation</h3>
  <p>
    You also need to document your home office itself:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Square footage:</strong> Measure your office space and total home</li>
    <li><strong>Photos:</strong> Show the dedicated workspace</li>
    <li><strong>Floor plan:</strong> Optional but helpful—sketch showing office location</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How Long to Keep Records</h3>
  <p>
    The IRS recommends keeping records for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>At least 3 years</strong> from filing date</li>
    <li><strong>7 years</strong> if you claimed a loss or underreported income</li>
    <li><strong>Life of the asset + 3 years</strong> for depreciated items</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pro tip:</strong> Use cloud storage (Google Drive, Dropbox) to keep digital copies.
    Create a folder for each tax year with subfolders for receipts.
  </p>

  <!-- Employee vs Self-Employed -->
  <h2 id="employee-vs-self-employed">Employee vs Self-Employed: Key Differences</h2>

  <h3>Self-Employed (1099, Freelance, Business Owner)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Can deduct:</strong> Home office expenses including furniture</li>
    <li><strong>Form:</strong> Schedule C (sole proprietor) or business return</li>
    <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> Regular and exclusive business use of space</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>W-2 Employees</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Federal deduction:</strong> Not available (suspended 2018-2025)</li>
    <li><strong>State deductions:</strong> Some states still allow (check local rules)</li>
    <li><strong>Employer reimbursement:</strong> Can ask employer to reimburse (tax-free to you)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Hybrid Situation</h3>
  <p>
    If you're an employee AND have side freelance income:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You can deduct home office expenses only for the freelance portion</li>
    <li>Prorate furniture based on hours spent on each activity</li>
    <li>Example: 20 hours/week freelance, 40 hours employee = 33% of furniture deductible</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Limits and Caps -->
  <h2 id="limits">Limits and Caps</h2>

  <h3>Section 179 Limits</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Total deduction limit:</strong> $1,160,000 (2024)</li>
    <li><strong>Phase-out threshold:</strong> Begins at $2,890,000 in purchases</li>
    <li><strong>Business income limit:</strong> Can't exceed your net business income</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For most freelancers, these limits are far above what you'd spend on furniture.
    They're more relevant for businesses buying vehicles and equipment.
  </p>

  <h3>Home Office Deduction Limits</h3>
  <p>
    If using the simplified home office method ($5/sq ft, max 300 sq ft):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Maximum deduction: $1,500</li>
    <li>Furniture is deducted separately (not included in $1,500)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If using the regular method (actual expenses):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>No cap on furniture deductions</li>
    <li>Total home office deduction can't exceed business income</li>
    <li>Excess can be carried forward to future years</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Practical Limits</h3>
  <p>
    While there's no hard cap on furniture deductions, unreasonably expensive items
    might raise audit flags. A $10,000 desk when you earn $50,000 might get questioned.
    Document the business necessity for high-cost items.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Conclusion: Deduct What You Deserve</h2>
  <p>
    Home office furniture is a legitimate business expense that reduces your taxes. The process
    is straightforward if you document properly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Your action checklist:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Take photos of your home office setup</li>
    <li>Measure and record square footage</li>
    <li>Save receipts for all furniture purchases (digital is fine)</li>
    <li>Note the business purpose for each item</li>
    <li>Use Section 179 for immediate deduction</li>
    <li>Consult a tax professional if your situation is complex</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Invest in a comfortable, productive workspace—and let the tax deduction make it more affordable.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
